{"schemaVersion":"1.0.0","commitSha":"c2534ff9c84422e00711da31629a76f3f59feb98","shard":"e86a869945dbaf9e","symbols":[{"path":"xrpl/Number/Guard","kind":"class","name":"Guard","qualifiedName":"xrpl::Number::Guard","scope":"xrpl/Number","category":"production","access":"public","documentation":{"status":"present","brief":[{"type":"link","text":"Guard","target":{"path":"xrpl/Number/Guard","resolution":"doxygen"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"description":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"link","text":"Guard","target":{"path":"xrpl/Number/Guard","resolution":"doxygen"}},{"type":"text","text":" class is used to temporarily add extra digits of precision to an operation. This enables the final result to be correctly rounded to the internal precision of "},{"type":"link","text":"Number","target":{"path":"xrpl/Number","resolution":"doxygen"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"At its core, the "},{"type":"link","text":"Guard","target":{"path":"xrpl/Number/Guard","resolution":"doxygen"}},{"type":"text","text":" really only needs three pieces of information to determine how to round:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":[[{"type":"text","text":"The rounding mode"}],[{"type":"text","text":"The last digit dropped from the mantissa (i.e. the first digit after the decimal point). (first byte of digits_)"}],[{"type":"text","text":"Whether any other non-zero digits were dropped from the mantissa. (remaining bytes of digits_ and xbit_)"}]]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upward and Downward rounding modes round the unsigned mantissa toward or away from zero depending on whether the sign is negative (sbit_). For positive values, Upward is away, and Downward is toward. For negative values, that's reversed. For simplicity, I'm going to describe the logic using \"TowardZero\" and \"AwayFromZero\"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"TowardZero is the easiest rounding mode. It always rounds down. digits_ and xbit_ are irrelevant. AwayFromZero is almost as simple. If both \"digits_\" and \"xbit_\" are zero (0), it rounds down. Else it rounds up. ToNearest is only a little more complicated. If the last dropped digit is < 5, then round down. If it is > 5, round up. If it is exactly 5, and there are "},{"type":"emphasis","text":"any"},{"type":"text","text":" other digits (the remainder of \"digits_\" or \"xbit_\"), round up, else round to even."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The current implementation stores 16 digits in \"digits_\" so that digits can be \"pop\"ped back out if needed during subtraction (negative addition) operations."}]}],"returns":null,"notes":[],"suppressionReason":null,"inheritedFrom":null},"location":{"declaration":{"file":"src/libxrpl/basics/Number.cpp","line":219},"definition":{"file":"src/libxrpl/basics/Number.cpp","line":220,"endLine":343}},"corrections":[],"templateParameters":null,"isTemplateSpecialization":false,"isFinal":false,"isAbstract":false,"baseClasses":[],"derivedClasses":[],"friends":[],"deductionGuides":[],"nested":[],"members":["xrpl/Number/Guard/Round","xrpl/Number/Guard/digits_","xrpl/Number/Guard/xbit_","xrpl/Number/Guard/sbit_","xrpl/Number/Guard/minMantissa","xrpl/Number/Guard/maxMantissa","xrpl/Number/Guard/cuspRoundingFix","xrpl/Number/Guard/Guard/60ae6518","xrpl/Number/Guard/Guard/d2dd1f26","xrpl/Number/Guard/setPositive/0db8786e","xrpl/Number/Guard/setNegative/e68890ff","xrpl/Number/Guard/setDropped/c728f062","xrpl/Number/Guard/isNegative/c28850c0","xrpl/Number/Guard/push/9bf3a2bc","xrpl/Number/Guard/pop/d3239448","xrpl/Number/Guard/unrecoverable/675fc026","xrpl/Number/Guard/empty/cd46a334","xrpl/Number/Guard/doDropDigit/de8f32e6","xrpl/Number/Guard/doDropDigitWithTarget/00430739","xrpl/Number/Guard/doRoundUp/c2a0b956","xrpl/Number/Guard/doRoundDown/80c75512","xrpl/Number/Guard/doRound/466c3a86","xrpl/Number/Guard/doDropDigit/645a47d7","xrpl/Numb